
Storage Classes

    What:
        A storage class defines the scope (visibility), lifetime, default initial value,
        and storage location (memory/register) of a variable in C.

    Why:
        To control how long a variable lives and from where it can be accessed.
        Helps in efficient memory management, data sharing across files, and optimisation.

    Where:
        Used while declaring variables inside functions, files, or across multiple source files.

    How:
        Specified before the data type in a declaration:
            storage_class data_type variable_name;

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    Four Storage Classes in C

        1. auto     (automatic)
        2. register
        3. static
        4. extern

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    1. auto

        • Default storage class for all local variables declared inside a function/block.
        • Stored on the stack.
        • Lifetime   : exists only while the block is executing.
        • Scope      : local to the block in which it is defined.
        • Default Value : garbage (uninitialised).
        • The keyword `auto` is rarely written explicitly.

        Example:
            #include <stdio.h>
            void func() {
                auto int x = 10;   // same as int x = 10;
                printf("%d\n", x);
            }
            int main() {
                func();
                // printf("%d", x);   // error – x not visible here
                return 0;
            }

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    2. register

        • Suggests the compiler store the variable in a CPU register for faster access.
        • It is a request, not a command – compiler may ignore it.
        • Cannot take the address of a register variable (no & operator allowed).
        • Scope & Lifetime : same as auto (local to block).
        • Default Value : garbage.
        • Typically used for loop counters or frequently accessed variables.

        Example:
            #include <stdio.h>
            void loop() {
                register int i;
                for (i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
                    printf("%d ", i);
                }
            }
            int main() {
                loop();
                return 0;
            }

        • The statement `int *p = &i;` would cause a compilation error if i is register.

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    3. static

        • Extends the lifetime of a variable to the entire program execution.
        • The variable is initialised only once and retains its value between function calls.
        • If used inside a function:
            - Scope remains local (visible only inside that function).
            - Lifetime is static (persists across calls).
        • If used outside all functions (global static):
            - Scope is limited to the current source file only (internal linkage).
        • Memory : stored in the data segment (initialised data or BSS).
        • Default Value : zero if not initialised explicitly.

        Example 1 – Static local variable:
            #include <stdio.h>
            void counter() {
                static int count = 0;
                count++;
                printf("%d ", count);
            }
            int main() {
                counter();   // 1
                counter();   // 2
                counter();   // 3
                return 0;
            }

        Example 2 – Static global variable:
            // file1.c
            static int hidden = 100;   // not visible in other files
            void show() {
                printf("%d\n", hidden);
            }

            // file2.c
            // extern int hidden;  -> linker error, hidden is not accessible

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    4. extern

        • Declares a variable that is defined in another source file (or later in the same file).
        • Used for sharing variables across multiple files.
        • The variable must be defined exactly once (without extern) somewhere in the program.
        • Lifetime : entire program execution.
        • Scope : global (visible in all files that declare it).
        • Memory : data segment.
        • Default Value : zero (when defined without initialiser).

        Example:
            // file1.c
            #include <stdio.h>
            int global = 50;          // definition
            void display();

            int main() {
                display();
                return 0;
            }

            // file2.c
            #include <stdio.h>
            extern int global;        // declaration (definition is in file1.c)
            void display() {
                printf("global = %d\n", global);
            }

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    Quick Comparison Table

    | Storage Class | Scope        | Lifetime         | Default Value | Memory     |
    |---------------|--------------|------------------|---------------|------------|
    | auto          | local        | block execution  | garbage       | stack      |
    | register      | local        | block execution  | garbage       | CPU reg    |
    | static        | local/file   | whole program    | zero          | data/BSS   |
    | extern        | global       | whole program    | zero          | data/BSS   |

    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    Additional Notes

        • The `static` keyword in a function definition limits the function's visibility to
          the file it is defined in (internal linkage).
            static int helper() { return 1; }   // not callable from other files

        • The `extern` keyword can also be used with functions, but it is optional since
          all functions are extern by default.

